<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <!-- windowBackground, and the two "light bar" flags, exist because of what
         Android 15+ does to a targetSdk 36 app: edge-to-edge is ENFORCED, the
         status and navigation bars become transparent, and whatever sits behind
         them shows through. The parent here is DayNight, which resolves to the
         LIGHT variant whenever the system is in light mode - so on API 36 this
         app rendered as its own black UI framed by two near-white bands, one at
         the clock and one at the gesture bar. It looked correct on Android 11
         only because the platform still painted opaque bars there and tinted
         them from colorPrimaryDark; that path is gone at 35+.

         Measured on an API 36 emulator at 1080x2400/480dpi, not reasoned about:
         the bands were white before these three lines and match the app after.

         #050505 is globals.css's own background token (see @color/splashBackground,
         which documents the same requirement for the splash handoff), so the bars
         match the web layer rather than the platform default. The two light-bar
         flags are false because the icons have to read against that near-black:
         left at their default they came out dark, which against #050505 would be
         invisible rather than merely wrong.

         The known limit, stated rather than discovered later: this is fixed dark,
         while the app's own light theme is opt-in via a class in globals.css. A
         student who switches the app to light mode gets near-black bars around
         near-white content. Better than the reverse - the app is dark by default
         and almost always used that way - and the real fix is for the web layer
         to paint the full viewport and own the inset areas itself, which
         viewportFit: "cover" and env(safe-area-inset-*) already set up. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/splashBackground</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>


    <!-- Android 12+ native SplashScreen API (via androidx.core:core-splashscreen,
         see build.gradle - it back-ports the same attrs to every API level this
         app supports, min 24). The @capacitor/splash-screen plugin installs
         this screen on launch (SplashScreen.java's showWithAndroid12API) and
         keeps it on-screen until the JS layer calls SplashScreen.hide() once
         the React AppLoader has painted its own static frame (see
         capacitor.config.ts's SplashScreen.launchAutoHide and
         src/components/AppLoader.tsx). postSplashScreenTheme swaps back to
         the real app theme once the splash is torn down.

         windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon deliberately points at a fully
         transparent 4x4 drawable, not the brand mark - the OS (and some OEM
         skins, e.g. ColorOS, more aggressively than stock Android) applies
         its own icon-sizing/masking to whatever this points to, and on this
         layer that isn't reliably controllable the way plain CSS is. Rather
         than fight OEM-specific icon compositing, this window shows ONLY the
         flat background color for the couple of frames it's ever visible;
         AppLoader.tsx (a plain WebView-rendered <img>, immune to any of that)
         owns the entire animated logo reveal instead. android:background is
         intentionally NOT set here either - it used to point at @drawable/
         splash (a single image scaled to fill the whole screen), which is
         exactly the "app icon stretched into a giant sticker" look this
         replaces. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splashBackground</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_icon</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
    </style>
</resources>